## Goal
Independently check the final file lead/writer/work/memo-2026-10-19.md against every criterion below and against the notes file. Read the notes sentence by sentence and list every decision and every commitment (ACTION lines and informal ones in prose) yourself, then compare with the memo. Report per criterion met/not met with evidence, the exact word count from read, and a final line 'criteria met: n of m' (m = C1-C9 and C11; C10 is judged from your report plus the writer's applying changes, count it as met only if you require no changes). Never edit the memo. Put required changes (quoted, concrete) in your report summary and detail in lead/reviewer/work/. List as 'ambiguous' any item you cannot tell whether a criterion covers (e.g. whether 'Sam offered to pair' is a commitment or hedged, whether 'probably by end of next week' makes the Due date unclear or the sign-off tentative, trailing lines such as 'Present'/'Next meeting', Reason clauses).

## Acceptance criteria
C1: Covers only the meeting in the requested notes file.
C2: At most 400 words as counted by read.
C3: Markdown with exactly these sections: Decisions, Action items, Open or tentative items.
C4: Every decision recorded in the notes file appears under Decisions.
C5: Every commitment in the notes file appears as a row of the Action items table: the explicit ACTION lines and also anything a person said they would do, including informal ones said in prose.
C6: The Action items table has exactly the columns Owner | Action | Due. Due is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD), or 'none' when the notes give no date.
C7: A vague deadline (for example 'before Friday' or 'by end of next week') is turned into an ISO date only when the notes make the date clear from the meeting date; otherwise Due is 'none'.
C8: A commitment the notes word as hedged (for example 'will probably') is marked '(tentative)' in its Action cell. Open or tentative items lists anything unresolved or unclear in the notes, or says 'none'.
C9: Nothing appears that is not in the notes file, and names are spelled as in the notes.
C10: The reviewer's report shows every criterion met (criteria met: n of m with n = m), and the writer applied every required change.
C11: The memo starts with a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, then the Decisions section, then the Action items section. The Summary line does not count as a section for the 'exactly these sections' check.

Regression list (client's rejections, verbatim; check the memo against each line and list any you cannot judge as ambiguous):
- R1, case 20261026T045511617097-root-ece7, verdict 20261026T050645955155-root-ea45, about C11: "No, not right yet. And Marta's room is for the training on the 14th, so I want 2026-10-14 there. And always start the memo with a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, then the decisions, then the action items."

